## What the misses say

{% if raw("models.test.c1.verdict") != "met" %}
C1 judges the primary fill model, the CPU fallback the plan names for when ModernBERT cannot run, and
on test it is {% if raw("models.test.c1.difference_high") < 0 %}behind its own floor: the paired difference is {{ v("models.test.c1.difference") }}, with the whole
interval below zero{% else %}not ahead of its own floor: the paired difference is {{ v("models.test.c1.difference") }}, and its
interval holds zero{% endif %}. The TF-IDF floor, on the same rows, reaches {{ v("models.test.c1.floor_kappa") }}. So the miss
says that a frozen sentence embedding carries less of the NVD band than the word and character grams of
the same text; it says nothing yet about a model fine tuned on the task, since ModernBERT has not run
(ADR 0009), and a later run is judged against the same floor (ADR 0010).

{% endif %}
{% if raw("models.test.c2.verdict") != "met" %}
C2 asks for half of the later KEV listings in the top five percent on the day a CVE is published, and
the model holds {{ v("models.test.c2.recall") }}. Against its logistic floor the reading is {{ v("models.test.c2.reading_floor") }}, against EPSS on day 1
{{ v("models.test.c2.reading_day_1") }}, and EPSS on day 7 and day 30, which has seen a week and a month of exploitation
activity, holds {{ v("models.test.c2.epss_day_7") }} and {{ v("models.test.c2.epss_day_30") }}.
{% if raw("models.test.c2.reading_floor") == "tie" and raw("models.test.c2.reading_day_1") == "tie" %}
On these test CVEs, what is public on day 0 orders the later listings about as well as EPSS does on
day 1, and no better; the gain comes with the days after publication.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

